.DS_Store
# Query results the CLI writes into the working directory, one folder per
# connection hash. Data extracted from a customer's warehouse has no business in
# version control.
query_results_*.csv
[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]*/

# Measurements from tools/load_probe.py. Useful while tuning the connection
# budget, meaningless once the machine that produced them is gone.
load_probe.jsonl

# Coverage
htmlcov/
.coverage
coverage.xml

# artifacts/ is tracked, by request. The screenshots and videos under it are
# regenerated on every run against a live stack, so they are derived files and
# each run rewrites them -- carrying them in history means every re-record adds
# its own copy of a few megabytes. That is a deliberate trade for being able to
# see the evidence without running the suite. Worth moving to Git LFS if the
# repository starts feeling slow to clone.

# The Q&A export is tracked, by request. Its answers do quote query results
# verbatim -- names and revenue figures -- which is why it was excluded before.
# It is defensible here because every warehouse behind it is a public sample
# database (chinook, northwind, pagila and friends), so the names are fictional.
# Point this repository at a real warehouse and the export stops being safe to
# commit: regenerate with `make qa-json` and re-add the `qa*.json` rule.

# Execution-accuracy reports from backend/evals/sql_accuracy.py
backend/evals/results/
# Registry, converted benchmarks, predictions and reports written by
# backend/evals/toolkit_bridge.py -- regenerated by `make eval-toolkit-export`.
.evals/
